Randomized invariant check (brute-force permutation over each committed set):

```go
func applySerial(state map[string]int64, ops []obsOp) bool {
	for _, o := range ops {
		switch o.kind {
		case obsPoint:
			if state[o.key] != o.val { return false }
		case obsRange:
			if !sameRows(rangeRows(state, o.key, "\xff"), o.rows) { return false }
		case obsWrite:
			state[o.key] = o.val
		}
	}
	return true
}

func TestRandomizedSerializable(t *testing.T) {
	for seedVal := int64(0); seedVal < 5000; seedVal++ { runRandom(t, seedVal) }
}
```

## 4. Verification

```bash
$ go test ./...
ok      mvcc    0.127s

$ go test -race ./...
ok      mvcc    1.457s

$ gofmt -l . && go vet ./...
# no output -> formatted and vet-clean
```

| Test | Scenario | Assertion |
|------|----------|-----------|
| `TestWriteSkewPoint` | `a` reads `x` writes `y`; `b` reads `y` writes `x` | both abort; exactly 2 aborts |
| `TestBenignConcurrent` | disjoint read/write sets | both commit, 0 aborts |
| `TestWriteSkewRangePhantom` | both `ReadRange` and insert new keys in range | both abort (phantom detected) |
| `TestOnlyCycleParticipantsAbort` | write-skew pair + independent txn | only the pair aborts; independent txn commits |
| `TestDangerousStructureWithReadDependency` | `t2-rw->t3`, `t1-rw->t2`, `t3-wr->t1`, `t3` commits first | first committer stays committed; `t2`/`t1` abort |
| `TestLostUpdate` | concurrent read–modify–write on one key | never both commit; result serially reproducible |
| `TestRandomizedSerializable` | 5000 random 3–5-way schedules, point + range reads | brute-force permutation checker always finds a reproducing serial order |

The randomized test is the strongest check of the stated invariant: for every committed set it tries **every serial permutation**, replaying each transaction's operations in order (with read-your-own-writes) and comparing every observed point read and range row-set. It passes under `-race`, confirming no schedule yields a non-serializable committed set and aborts are confined to cycle participants.
